<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ezzonids</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ezzonids were a noble dynasty of Lotharingia, named after Ezzo, count palatine of Lotharingia (died 1034), who married Matilda, daughter of Emperor Otto II. They held the palatine office in Lotharingia in the eleventh century, with lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, and members became dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and archbishops of Cologne. Pressure from the archbishops of Cologne later pushed the counts palatine south towards the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family first appears with Erenfried I (866–904), count of the Bliesgau, Keldachgau and Bonngau and perhaps also of the Charmois. He may have had Carolingian ancestors, though some historians prefer to link him to the former Thuringian kings. The dynasty's rise becomes visible in the second half of the tenth century, as it acquired one county after another; it came to rule most of the Rhenish counties and was granted palatine status over the other counts of the district.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the Brauweiler chronicle, Ezzo failed to win the crown after the death of Emperor Otto III, in a rivalry with Duke Henry II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. The conflict lasted over ten years and ended in an agreement after a battle at Odernheim in 1011; for renouncing the throne Ezzo received Kaiserswerth, Duisburg and the surrounding imperial lands as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>. Otto I, count palatine from 1035, campaigned successfully against the rebel count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and in 1045 received the duchy of Swabia, giving Kaiserswerth and Duisburg back to the crown in exchange.</p><p>Other members rose high under the Salian emperors. Conrad I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, died in exile after an attempt to assassinate Emperor Henry III and seize the throne, and Conrad III held the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>. Richeza of Lotharingia became queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as the wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-lambert">Mieszko II Lambert</a>. The last count palatine of the line, Hermann II (1064–1085), saw his power much reduced by his guardian, Anno II, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. After his death at Dalhem on 20 September 1085 the Palatinate of Lotharingia was suspended, and his widow married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-laach">Henry of Laach</a>, the first count palatine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Although they usually represented royal interests and served the emperors in war, the Ezzonids never built a territorial state in Lotharingia. Their line probably survived in the counts of Limburg Stirum, who are believed to descend from Adolf I of Lotharingia, youngest son of Hermann I. A cadet branch ruled as Counts of Berg, among the most powerful dynasties of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and Adolf I, Count of the Mark, of a collateral Berg line, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-marck">House of La Marck</a>.</p><p>The House of Limburg-Stirum, which took its name in the 12th century from the immediate county of Limburg an der Lenne, is the eldest and only surviving branch of the House of Berg. In the church, Hermann I, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, served as chancellor to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwentibold">Zwentibold of Lotharingia</a>, and Hermann II, Archbishop of Cologne, as chancellor for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ezzonids” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Lotharingia it was held in the eleventh century by the <b>Ezzonid</b> family, with lands around Aachen and Cologne, but pressure from the archbishops of Cologne pushed the counts palatine southwards to the middle Rhine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
